S.append(grid([[P("Hero's level", cellhead), P("HP", cellhead), P("AC", cellhead), P("The sword", cellhead), P("Attacks", cellhead), P("Action Surge", cellhead), P("Indomitable", cellhead), P("Dice", cellhead), P("", cellhead)]] + [[P(a, cellb)] + [P(x, cell) for x in r] for a, *r in rows], [0.75*inch, 0.45*inch, 0.4*inch, 1.0*inch, 0.6*inch, 0.85*inch, 0.8*inch, 0.95*inch, W-5.8*inch]))

S.append(P("Snapshots, with the feats checked", h2)); S.append(rule())
def snap(name, where, lvl, lines, checked, label="The feat, checked."):
    content = [P(name, snapname), P(where + ("  ·  " + lvl if lvl else ""), stattag)] + [P(l, snapline) for l in lines] + [P(f"<b>{label}</b> " + checked, snapline)]
    t = Table([[content]], colWidths=[W]); t.setStyle(TableStyle([("BACKGROUND",(0,0),(-1,-1),PARCH),("LINEBEFORE",(0,0),(0,-1),2.5,GREEN),("LEFTPADDING",(0,0),(-1,-1),10),("RIGHTPADDING",(0,0),(-1,-1),10),("TOPPADDING",(0,0),(-1,-1),7),("BOTTOMPADDING",(0,0),(-1,-1),7)]))
    return KeepTogether([t, Spacer(1,7)])
S.append(snap("The room without walls", "The Water Temple, Ocarina of Time: the only place it has ever stood", "The hero's level 9",
 ["<b>AC</b> 20 · <b>HP</b> 103 · two attacks at +12 for 1d8+10 · one Action Surge, one Indomitable · five d8s, DC 17 · translucent for the first ten rounds"],
 "The game's numbers: it has two points of health for every heart container the hero owns, a Master Sword slash takes two and a jump slash four, and its own sword takes half a heart from him. So it needs twice as many hits as he does, and the fight is the hero's to lose. On these sheets his Master Sword does 1d8+10 and 2d8 radiant, about 23, into 103 hit points, and the copy does about 14 into his: five hits against eight, close to the game's two to one once the Mirror has eaten its share. The game's known answers all come out of the rules without being written in. <b>The Deku Nut</b> stuns it, and a stunned shadow can't mirror or sink. <b>The Megaton Hammer</b> and <b>Biggoron's Sword</b> take two hands, so there's nothing for it to copy; in the game it raises its shield at the hammer and is hit anyway, and Biggoron's Sword does double. <b>Din's Fire</b> is a spell, so the roll doesn't help; in the game it stands in the fire with its shield up. <b>Arrows and bombs</b> are what it rolls away from, in both. And a hero who stabs or jumps at it gets the Answer, which is the part of the fight everyone remembers."))
S.append(snap("The shadow on the sand", "The arena: the hero in his prime walks in, and so does this", "The hero's level 20",
 ["The headline block: AC 20, 224 HP, four attacks, the six traits. It walks in solid: the arena has no room and no minute to wait"],
 "Against the Hero of Time it loses every time, in three or four rounds: he has the nuts, Nayru's Love, the fairies and a sword that was made for exactly this. Across the ladder it wins about three fights in ten, fifteenth of eighteen, and who it beats follows from the game. It beats what it can copy or roll away from: Talion and his one-handed sword nine times in ten, Arthur Morgan's revolvers nine in ten, Spike and Goblin Slayer every time, and Itachi about three times in five, because there's no mind in it for a genjutsu to hold. It loses to what it can't copy: Guts and the Dragonslayer five times in six, and Jin's two-handed katana, Crane's claws and V more than nine times in ten. Musashi, whose swords it can copy, beats it nine times in ten anyway, and it all but never beats the Slayer, Anderson, Guy, Smasher, Battle Beast or Kratos. Two fighters change weapons for it: Kratos leaves the Axe for the Blades on their chains, and V takes the katana in both hands. Put back in its own room, translucent for ten rounds, it beats only the two who can't kill it in a minute, Spike and Goblin Slayer; everyone else is through its 224 hit points before it has a blade to hit them with."))
S.append(snap("Other shadows", "Other games, other heroes: notes, not sheets", "",
 ["Several heroes have met one. The Reflection rule builds any of them from that hero's sheet; what differs is who made it and what it's for"],
 "<b>Link's Shadow</b> (<i>The Adventure of Link</i>, 1987) is the first: the last trial of the Great Palace, stepping out of the hero's own shadow between him and the Triforce of Courage. It is the purest case of the rule, a duel with no tricks in it but his own. <b>Shadow Link</b> (<i>Four Swords Adventures</i>) is a different creature and would need a different sheet: <i>Hyrule Historia</i> says they are demons born of the Dark Mirror, there are many of them, and they do things on their own account. The four <b>Dark Links</b> of the Palace of the Four Sword (<i>A Link to the Past</i>, the Game Boy Advance version) each fight with a different one of the hero's techniques. Veran's copies of the hero (<i>Oracle of Ages</i>), the dark figures in Lanayru's vision (<i>Twilight Princess</i>), the last opponent of <i>Spirit Tracks</i>' gauntlet and the StreetPass shadows of <i>A Link Between Worlds</i> are all the same idea with less on the page. <i>Hyrule Warriors</i> and <i>Super Smash Bros.</i> are outside the canon this sheet uses, and in <i>Breath of the Wild</i> it's a suit of armor.", label="The others."))

S.append(KeepTogether([P("A DM's dial", h2), rule(), P("Four knobs, the most generous first. <b>The level.</b> The game only ever shows this shadow against a hero at about the midpoint of his story. Scaling it to the hero in front of it is the health rule taken at its word; if you'd rather stat only what was on screen, it's the ninth-level block and it stays there. <b>The Fighter's features.</b> The first draft of this sheet copied the body, the sword and the shield and nothing else: no Action Surge, no dice, no Indomitable. It beat Spike and Goblin Slayer and lost to everyone else on the ladder nearly every time. Copying the hero's training along with his arm is a ruling, and it's the one that makes it a fight. <b>Mirror.</b> Limiting it to one-handed weapons is this sheet's reading of why the hammer and Biggoron's Sword are the game's easy answers. Let it mirror any sword and it gets a good deal harder for Guts and Jin. <b>Creature of evil.</b> If your table reads it as a neutral trial, the Master Sword loses 2d8 a hit against it and the hero's fight gets longer, though not much more dangerous. One more, with no canon behind it at all: the room makes a shadow of whoever walks in. Apply the Reflection to Guts.")]))
doc.build(S, onFirstPage=on_page, onLaterPages=on_page); print("ok")
